[
  {
    "id": 0,
    "title": "Level 1: Birth of your PyPet",
    "concept": "Strings and variables",
    "stages": [
      {
        "id": 0,
        "title": "Waking Up",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "Hello! I am CHU (Codified Helper Unit). But you can call me Professor Chu!",
              "I'm here to help you learn how to care for your new PyPet.",
              "Let's wake it up. Click on the egg a few times until it hatches."
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "click_target",
            "targetClicks": 5
          }
        ],
        "isReplEnabled": false
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "title": "Say Hello",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "Yay, your new PyPet is awake!",
              "Why don't we try to say hello? Type `Hello` into the terminal on the right."
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "repl_test",
            "replChecks": [
              {
                "type": "exactMatch",
                "accept": [
                  "Hello",
                  "hello"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "isReplEnabled": true
      },
      {
        "id": 2,
        "title": "What is Python",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "Oof, it doesn't look like your PyPet liked that...",
              "That's because your PyPet only knows the programming language **Python**!",
              "**Python**, just like any language, has specific words, and rules for how to use those words. You'll need to learn some of those rules to properly care for your PyPet... That's what I'm here for.",
              "Okay, your first Python word..... *drum roll*.... `print`. Type this into the terminal now."
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "repl_test",
            "replChecks": [
              {
                "type": "exactMatch",
                "accept": [
                  "print"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "isReplEnabled": true
      },
      {
        "id": 3,
        "title": "print()",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "So, what happened?\nYou should see `<built-in function print>` in the terminal, but what does this mean?",
              "Well, this tells you that `print` is a **function**, and that it's built into **Python**, that means it's part of the language. But what is a **function**?",
              "A **function** is a named set of instructions (you'll get to write your own **function** soon.)\nFor now, what you need to know is that to **call** a **function** you must include brackets. Sometimes we'll also put data inside the brackets.",
              "Try this, type `print(\"Hello\")` into the terminal."
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "repl_test",
            "replChecks": [
              {
                "type": "matchOutput",
                "accept": [
                  "Hello",
                  "'Hello'",
                  "\"Hello\""
                ],
                "ignoreCase": true
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "isReplEnabled": true
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": 1,
    "title": "Level 2: Memory",
    "concept": "Variables",
    "stages": [
      {
        "id": 0,
        "title": "Variables",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "Well done! You've learned how to use the print **function**.",
              "Next up, let's give your PyPet a name! To do that we have to learn about **variables**.",
              "**Variables** are a way to store **data**, you can think of it like a box, the **variable name** is the label on the box, the data is what you put inside the box.",
              "Open the Codepad and we'll start making some variables!.."
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "open_codepad"
          },
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "Welcome to the Codepad! Here we will write code that will stay in your pet's memory.",
              "We're going to start creating your pet's memory by defining three **variables** (boxes of data):",
              "**Variable names** have rules too. **Variable names** must start with a letter and can't contain any spaces. We can put data into **variables** using the **assignment operator** `=`, you might know it as equals.",
              "Copy this code into your codepad\n\n`pet_name = \"Ada\"` \n`pet_type = \"PyPet\"`\n`pet_age = 0`\n\nand then click [Run Tests]"
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "codepad_test",
            "tests": [
              {
                "id": 1,
                "text": "Define pet_name",
                "check": "variableValueMatches",
                "args": {
                  "name": "pet_name",
                  "expectedValue": "Ada"
                },
                "hint": "Make sure you typed pet_name = \"Ada\""
              },
              {
                "id": 2,
                "text": "Define pet_type",
                "check": "variableValueMatches",
                "args": {
                  "name": "pet_type",
                  "expectedValue": "PyPet"
                },
                "hint": "Make sure you typed pet_type = \"PyPet\""
              },
              {
                "id": 3,
                "text": "Define pet_age",
                "check": "variableValueMatches",
                "args": {
                  "name": "pet_age",
                  "expectedValue": 0
                },
                "hint": "Make sure you typed pet_age = 0 without quotes, because it is a number."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "save_and_deploy"
          }
        ],
        "isReplEnabled": true
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "title": "Say my name",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "Well done. You've named your PyPet Ada!",
              "Let's test it out. Type `print(pet_name)` in the terminal."
