  {
    "id": 2,
    "title": "Level 3: The Blueprint",
    "concept": "Functions without Parameters",
    "stages": [
      {
        "id": 0,
        "title": "Trying an Undefined Function",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "Ada's memory only contains the code we give her. Right now, she doesn't know how to do any tricks!",
              "To teach her a trick, we use a **function**. Remember when you used the built-in `print` **function**? Now you get to make your own!",
              "Think of **defining** a **function** like writing a blueprint. Before you can **call** it you have to build it.",
              "Let's see what happens if you try to **call** a **function** that doesn't exist yet. Type `sleep()` into the terminal."
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "repl_test",
            "replChecks": [
              {
                "type": "exactMatch",
                "accept": [
                  "sleep()"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "isReplEnabled": true
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "title": "Defining Functions",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "See? Python gave you an error because the `sleep()` **function** hasn't been **defined** yet.",
              "Let's open the Codepad and write the blueprints for Ada's first tricks!"
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "open_codepad"
          },
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "To build a blueprint, we use the `def` keyword. This tells **Python** we are **defining** a new **function**.",
              "Notice the colon `:` at the end of the line? That tells **Python** that the instructions for this function are about to start.",
              "The instructions inside the **function** must be **indented** (pushed in with the Tab key) so **Python** knows they belong to that **function**.",
              "Copy this code into your codepad to define two functions:\n\n```def sleep():\n  print(\"Zzz\")\n\ndef wake():\n  print(\"Yawn\")```\n\nThen click [Run Tests]."
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "codepad_test",
            "tests": [
              {
                "id": 1,
                "text": "Define sleep() function",
                "check": "functionExists",
                "args": {
                  "name": "sleep"
                },
                "hint": "Start with: def sleep():"
              },
              {
                "id": 2,
                "text": "Check sleep() output matches \"Zzz\"",
                "check": "functionOutputMatches",
                "args": {
                  "name": "sleep",
                  "expectedOutput": "Zzz"
                },
                "hint": "Inside sleep(), print(\"Zzz\")",
                "unlocksMood": "sleeping"
              },
              {
                "id": 3,
                "text": "Define wake() function that prints \"Yawn\"",
                "check": "functionOutputMatches",
                "args": {
                  "name": "wake",
                  "expectedOutput": "Yawn"
                },
                "hint": "Inside wake(), print(\"Yawn\")",
                "unlocksMood": "idle"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "save_and_deploy"
          }
        ],
        "isReplEnabled": true
      },
      {
        "id": 2,
        "title": "Testing the Functions",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "Brilliant! You've successfully added two new **functions** to Ada's memory.",
              "Now that the **functions** have been **defined**, you can use the terminal to **call** them.",
              "Type `sleep()` in the terminal to **call** the function and tell Ada to go to sleep."
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "repl_test",
            "replChecks": [
              {
                "type": "exactMatch",
                "accept": [
                  "sleep()"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "devSolutionCode": "def sleep():\n  print(\"Zzz\")\ndef wake():\n  print(\"Yawn\")",
        "isReplEnabled": true
      },
      {
        "id": 3,
        "title": "And up again",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "Look at her go! When you called the `sleep()` **function**, the code inside it ran, printing \"Zzz\".",
              "Now let's wake her up. Call your `wake()` **function** in the terminal."
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "repl_test",
            "replChecks": [
              {
                "type": "matchOutput",
                "accept": [
                  "Yawn",
                  "'Yawn'",
                  "\"Yawn\""
                ],
                "ignoreCase": false
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "isReplEnabled": true
      },
      {
        "id": 4,
        "title": "Freeplay",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "Great job! You can now use `sleep()` and `wake()` any time you want.",
              "Play around with them in the terminal. When you're ready to learn how to make your functions even smarter, move on to the next level!"
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": false
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "freeplay"
          }
        ],
        "isReplEnabled": true
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": 3,
    "title": "Level 4: Blank Spaces",
    "concept": "Functions with Parameters",
    "stages": [
      {
        "id": 0,
        "title": "Concatenation",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "**Functions** are cool, but they become much more powerful when we can pass specific data into them.",
              "Before we upgrade our functions, we need to learn a trick with **strings** (text). We can glue them together using the `+` symbol! Programmers call this **concatenation**.",
              "Try gluing two strings together. Type `print(\"Good\" + \"morning\")` in the terminal."
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "repl_test",
            "replChecks": [
              {
                "type": "exactMatch",
                "accept": [
                  "print(\"Good\"+\"morning\")",
                  "print(\"Good\" + \"morning\")",
                  "print('Good'+'morning')",
                  "print('Good' + 'morning')"
                ],
                "ignoreCase": false
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "isReplEnabled": true
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "title": "Concatenation with spaces",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "Awesome! Notice how Python glued them into a single string: 'Goodmorning'.",
              "If we want to make sure there is a space between the words we need to include that in the data of the **string**. Try typing `print(\"Good \" + \"morning\")` in the terminal. (notice the space at the end of \"Good\"."
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "repl_test",
            "replChecks": [
              {
                "type": "matchOutput",
                "accept": [
                  "Good morning"
                ],
                "ignoreCase": false
              },
              {
                "type": "commandContains",
                "accept": [
                  "+"
                ],
                "ignoreCase": false
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "isReplEnabled": true
      },
      {
        "id": 2,
        "title": "Adding Parameters",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "Awesome! Always remember to add spaces when **concatenating** two or more **strings** together.",
              "Now, let's learn about **parameters**. A **parameter** is like a blank space inside your **function**. When you **call** the **function**, you pass data into that blank space!",
              "Open your Codepad to upgrade Ada's tricks."
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "open_codepad"
          },
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "Let's write a new function: `def greet(name):`",
              "See the word `name` inside the brackets? That's our **parameter**. It acts just like a **variable**, but it gets its data when we **call** the function!",
              "Inside the function, glue the strings together: `print(\"Hello \" + name)`"
            ],
            "blockFinalMessage": true
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "codepad_test",
            "tests": [
              {
                "id": 1,
                "text": "greet function is defined",
                "check": "functionExists",
                "args": {
                  "name": "greet"
                },
                "hint": "Don't forget to use the `def` keyword, followed by the function name `greet`, and then brackets and a colon `():`"
              },
              {
                "id": 2,
                "text": "greet function has exactly 1 parameter",
                "check": "functionParamsCount",
                "args": {
                  "name": "greet",
                  "expectedCount": 1
                },
                "hint": "Make sure your greet function has exactly one parameter, like this: def greet(name):"
              },
              {
                "id": 3,
                "text": "greet function output matches exactly",
                "check": "functionOutputMatches",
                "args": {
                  "name": "greet",
                  "testArgs": [
                    "Sarah"
                  ],
                  "mockInputs": [],
                  "mockRandoms": [],
                  "expectedOutput": "Hello Sarah"
                },
                "hint": "Make sure you print(\"Hello \" + name), don't forget the space at the end of Hello"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "save_and_deploy"
          }
        ],
        "isReplEnabled": true
      },
      {
        "id": 3,
        "title": "Sleep Parameter",
        "steps": [
          {
            "type": "action",
            "actionType": "open_codepad"
          },
          {
            "type": "dialogue",
            "messages": [
              "Excellent! Your `greet` function is working perfectly.",
              "Now, update your old `sleep` function to use a **parameter** too:\n\n```def sleep(name):\n  print(\"Goodnight \" + name + \" Zzz\")```\n\nClick [Run Tests] when you're done."
